alcemia are concerning but requires imaging confirmation"], "alarm_features": ["Hypercalcemia (12.5 mg/dL)", "Unintentional weight loss", "Neurological symptoms (trouble focusing)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Endocrine Hypercalcemia", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.3534834651113855, "posterior": 0.33977914744495313, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Normal physical exam weakly disfavors malignancy but does not rule it out given weight loss and hypercalcemia."], "evidence_against": ["Normal bone disease history with elevated ALP moderately disfavors endocrine hypercalcemia while supporting malignancy/reactive causes."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Malignancy with Bone Involvement", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.37192521385521127, "posterior": 0.15713287977491888, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Normal bone disease history with elevated ALP moderately disfavors endocrine hypercalcemia while supporting malignancy/reactive causes."], "evidence_against": ["Normal physical exam weakly disfavors malignancy but does not rule it out given weight loss and hypercalcemia."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 3.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Reactive Hypercalcemia / Nutritional Toxin", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.502, "danger": 0.3, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Systemic Inflammatory / Autoimmune Disease", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.001, "danger": 0.5, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}}, "frontier": ["B1", "B2"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the elevated alkaline phosphatase (150 U/L) and lack of osteoporosis/renal stone history disfavor endocrine hypercalcemia (e.g., primary hyperparathyroidism)?", "summary": "Elevated alkaline phosphatase is more typical of malignancy with bone involvement or vitamin D toxicity than primary hyperparathyroidism. This weakly supports malignancy and reactive hypercalcemia while modestly disfavoring endocrine hypercalcemia."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute onset of symptoms (1 week cough, worsening abdominal pain, neurological changes) with unintentional weight loss argue against malignancy as the primary explanation for hypercalcemia?", "summary": "Weight loss and neurological changes are concerning for malignancy, but the subacute time course weakens the case for cancer-driven hypercalcemia. This provides weak against malignancy while supporting endocrine and reactive causes."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the indirect reasoning chain linking unintentional weight loss to hypermetabolic state from hypercalcemia (e.g., polyuria, fatigue) support endocrine hypercalcemia as the primary mechanism?", "summary": "The subacute progression without acute-phase markers weakly supports endocrine hypercalcemia but does not strongly favor it."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the elevated alkaline phosphatase (150 U/L) in the context of hypercalcemia and subacute neurological symptoms strongly support malignancy with bone involvement as the underlying mechanism?", "summary": "Elevated ALP without osteolytic features provides moderate support for malignancy with bone involvement but less than definitive."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the elevated alkaline phosphatase (150 U/L) without osteolytic features or bone disease history disfavor endocrine hypercalcemia as the primary explanation for this case?", "summary": "Normal bone disease history with elevated ALP moderately disfavors endocrine hypercalcemia while supporting malignancy/reactive causes."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute symptom progression over days with normal physical exam findings disfavor malignancy as the primary explanation for this hypercalcemia?", "summary": "Normal physical exam weakly disfavors malignancy but does not rule it out given weight loss and hypercalcemia."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B2", 0.372], ["B1", 0.353], ["B3", 0.267]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No universal instability markers (airway, respiratory compromise, shock, or severe altered mental status) are present. Vital signs are stable, and symptoms are subacute without immediate life-threatening features.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading branch posterior (0.372) < 0.75, and dangerous alternative B2 remains unresolved with no definitive discriminators applied. No info exhaustion trigger met (PTH testing/CT imaging are untried discriminators)."}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.5, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.37192521385521127, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely explanation of this patient's current presentation?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Antacid overuse"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Increased 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Increased parathyroid hormone"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Malign